11:12:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's worth digging into, because "the body converts everything into energy through metabolism" is technically true but is doing a lot of sneaky work depending on how "metabolism" is being used. Two different meanings are getting mashed together: **Metabolism actually means ALL chemical processes in the body, not just energy production.** It splits into two halves: - **Catabolism**: breaking things down for energy (this is the part you're thinking of) - **Anabolism**: building things up—synthesizing proteins, DNA, hormones, cell membranes, neurotransmitters So when a doctor says "everything gets converted through metabolism," that's just... true, but it's not saying "everything becomes interchangeable fuel." It's saying "everything gets processed by your body's chemistry," which includes both burning things AND building things. The doctor's statement, taken at face value, doesn't actually contradict anything I've said—it's just a broader, vaguer claim that sounds like it's confirming your point if you don't know the anabolic/catabolic distinction. **Also worth asking: what was the article actually about?** Doctors say "the body converts food into energy through metabolism" all the time as a simplification for a specific point they're making—often something like: - Debunking myths that eating fat directly makes you fat (it doesn't automatically; it depends on energy balance) - Explaining basal metabolic rate - Correcting a specific misconception about a fad diet If you can share the actual article or quote, I can tell you more precisely what claim they were making and whether it really supports "food type doesn't matter." My strong suspicion is the quote is accurate in its narrow context but is being stretched by you (or maybe by the framing of the article itself) to mean something much broader than intended—kind of like how "everything is made of chemicals" is true but doesn't mean bleach and water are equivalent. What's the source? I'd genuinely like to see the exact wording.
