11:15:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is worth taking seriously, but there's a big gap between "what people said" and "what people actually did"—and that gap explains a lot. **Your grandparents' generation didn't have to think about food quality because their environment did it for them** - Ultra-processed food as we know it barely existed until the 1980s-2000s. There was no aisle of hyperpalatable, engineered snack food designed to override satiety signals. - Portion sizes were dramatically smaller (a 1950s soda was 6.5 oz; today's standard is 20 oz). - Seed oils, high-fructose corn syrup, and industrial food additives weren't ubiquitous ingredients yet. - They cooked more meals at home from whole ingredients by necessity, not philosophy. - Physical labor was far more common—manual jobs, walking, less mechanized daily life. Higher activity offsets a lot of dietary imperfection. So "they said a calorie is a calorie and didn't worry about it" isn't evidence that food type doesn't matter—it's evidence that **their food supply was accidentally closer to what nutritionists now recommend anyway**, so the distinction rarely came up as an issue. They didn't need the theory because their circumstances made it moot. **Survivorship bias is also doing real work here** You're hearing about the grandparents who lived long healthy lives. You don't hear as much about the ones in that generation who died of heart disease, stroke, or diabetes at 55—which happened plenty. Life expectancy has actually *increased* since their era, largely due to medical advances, but chronic disease rates from diet did exist then too (heart disease was already the leading cause of death by the 1950s-60s in the US). **And here's the ironic part** The generation that said "a calorie is a calorie" is also the same generation whose eating patterns—more whole foods, less processed food, smaller portions, more activity—would be recommended *by the very framework you're pushing back against*. Their words dismissed food quality; their actual lived behavior modeled it. The outcome supports the composition argument, not against it, once you look at what they were actually eating rather than what slogan they repeated.
